// NEGATIVE TEST — `==` on two Strings, PD0080. The well-typed-but-WRONG half.
//
// A String is a `const char*` in C and code generation emitted C's `==`, so the
// comparison was of two ADDRESSES. gcc accepted it and the program answered
// wrong with no diagnostic anywhere: on d5166e8 this printed 0. Two identical
// literals only looked right because gcc pools them into one address. The
// refusal points at the builtin that compares characters, `string_eq`.
fn main() {
    let s = int_to_string(12);
    if s == "12" {
        print_int(1);
    } else {
        print_int(0);
    }
}
